  "parent": "x-clade-east2280",
  "notes": "Lithuanian, the oldest attested Baltic language. Retains many archaic Indo-European features. Long vowels marked with macron/ogonek. Consonants are palatalized before front vowels (e ę ė i į y) and before the silent-i digraphs ia/ią/io/iu/iū that mark palatalization before a back vowel (Bakšienė et al. 2024) -- e.g. čia is /tʃʲa/, not /tʃia/. ą ę į ų mark historical vowel length, not nasality, in the modern language (Bakšienė et al. 2024; Ambrazas 1997, ch. on phonology). The standard orthography does not mark the acute/circumflex pitch-accent contrast on long syllables (Bakšienė et al. 2024), so it is not modelled here -- word-level tone is lexical information the letters do not carry. Digraphs dž and dz precede single letters in processing order. Ambrazas 1997 was consulted only via its published review (Language 76(2), 1997, DOI 10.2307/417422) and its own front-matter description, not the full 802pp. volume; its citation here backs the general grammar's existence and scope, not page-pinpointed claims.",
  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "baksiene_et_al_2024",
      "author": "Bakšienė, R., Čepaitienė, A., Jaroslavienė, J. & Urbanavičienė, J.",
      "year": 2024,
      "title": "Standard Lithuanian",
      "publisher": "Journal of the International Phonetic Association 54(1), 414-444",
      "doi": "10.1017/S0025100323000105",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100323000105",
      "notes": "Illustration of the IPA article. Its abstract states explicitly the palatalized/non-palatalized consonant opposition, the long/short vowel contrast and the pitch-accent system that motivate this spec's palatalization rules and its treatment of ą/ę/į/ų as length markers."
    },
    {
      "id": "ambrazas1997",
      "author": "Ambrazas, V. (ed.)",
      "year": 1997,
      "title": "Lithuanian Grammar",
      "publisher": "Vilnius: Baltos Lankos",
      "pages": null,
      "notes": "802pp. reference grammar; existence, title, editor, year and publisher confirmed via its published review (Language 76(2), 1997, DOI 10.2307/417422), full text not directly retrieved -- cited here for the grammar's general description of the palatalization, vowel-length and pitch-accent systems, not for page-pinpointed claims."
    }
  ],
